\subsection{Multi-Model Validation}
\label{sec:cross-model}

A Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct~\citep{llama3-2024} cross-model replication across all 11 task types ($N{=}220$) confirms that death dominance replicates across model families: internal death rate $41.4\%$ vs.\ deterministic $11.4\%$ (death-rate differential $+30.0$~pp), DAF $= 88.2\%$ (95\% CI: $[80.2\%, 95.1\%]$; $\Delta_{\text{full}} = +33.17$, $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +3.9$), with direction consistency 10/10 on death-prone tasks, 83 deterministic-preferred, 3 internal-preferred (1.4\%, all death-asymmetry reversals), and 134 no-preference labels.
Both models exhibit nearly identical internal vulnerability (${\sim}$42\%: Qwen $42.9\%$, Llama $41.4\%$), confirming that death-prone failure is structurally determined; the differential magnitude varies ($+26.5$~pp Qwen, $+30.0$~pp Llama) because deterministic strategy safety is model-dependent ($16.3\%$ vs.\ $11.4\%$).
\texttt{identify-life-stages-1} shows cross-model precision matching ($55\%$/$10\%$/$+45$~pp in both models).
\texttt{find-non-living-thing} shows zero deaths in both models, consistent with its majority-class answer distribution (\S\ref{sec:discussion}). Cross-model routing transfer is vacuously correct: Llama produces zero internal-preferred episodes, so Qwen-derived routing rules (always-deterministic) trivially match, confirming that routing signal is environment-determined rather than model-specific.
A third model family, Gemma-2-9B-it~\citep{gemma2-2024} (sliding window attention, vs.\ full attention in Qwen/Llama), independently confirms the pattern: DAF${=}89.7\%$ (95\% CI $[79.5\%, 98.4\%]$; $N{=}220$), with all 10 death-prone task types showing positive $\Delta$DR (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-model}). Three architecturally distinct models thus converge on DAF $88$--$90\%$ (spread 2.2~pp); $10{\times}$ scaling to 72B (\S\ref{sec:agent-improvement}) confirms persistence across capability levels.

\subsection{Agent Improvement Baselines}
\label{sec:agent-improvement}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrr@{}}
\toprule
Condition & Death\% & $N$ \\
\midrule
Single-trial baseline & 42.9 & 245 \\
Reflexion ($3{\times}$) & 31.4 & 220 \\
Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$) & 30.6 & 255 \\
Deterministic baseline & 16.3 & 245 \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{3}{@{}l}{\textit{10 death-prone tasks only:}} \\
Death-prone baseline & 48.0 & 200 \\
Retry ($5{\times}$) & 43.5 & 200 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Internal-strategy death rates under agent improvement methods. Top: all 11 tasks (72B: independently collected at both internal and deterministic). Bottom: 10 death-prone tasks (\texttt{find-non-living-thing} excluded, 0\% death at all scales). All interventions reduce death rate but none approaches deterministic-level safety.}
\label{tab:agent-improvement}
\end{table}

We test whether agent-level improvement reduces death dominance (Table~\ref{tab:agent-improvement}). Retry ($N{=}5$ attempts without self-correction) yields marginal reduction on death-prone tasks ($43.5\%$ vs.\ $48.0\%$ baseline, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp). Reflexion~\citep{reflexion-2023} (verbal self-reflection between trials) reduces death to 31.4\% across all 11 tasks by rescuing 29.3\% of first-trial deaths, but the gap to deterministic remains 15.1~pp; on the 10 death-prone tasks, Reflexion achieves ${\sim}$35\%. Under Reflexion ($N{=}3$, matched-episode analysis), $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = -0.47$, yielding DAF $> 100\%$: the deterministic advantage is entirely attributable to death avoidance, and in non-death episodes the self-correcting agent slightly outperforms the deterministic baseline. Scaling to Qwen2.5-72B ($10{\times}$ parameters; both internal and deterministic strategies independently collected at 72B, 255 episodes across all 11 task types) reduces death rate to $30.6\%$. The 72B deterministic strategy achieves $2.75\%$ death rate ($\Delta\text{DR} = +27.8$~pp), and $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.28$, yielding DAF $= 92.4\%$ (7B: $90.4\%$); scaling lowers death rate but leaves death dominance intact.

\paragraph{Dose-response analysis.}
We extend both interventions to $N{=}5$ trials on death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$; Figure~\ref{fig:dose-response}). Retry is nearly flat ($48.0\% \to 43.5\%$, $\Delta = -4.5$~pp over five trials). Reflexion drops steeply to $34.0\%$ at $N{=}3$ ($\Delta = -13.5$~pp, $z = 2.75$, $p = 0.003$) then plateaus ($34$--$36.5\%$ for $N \geq 3$, pairwise $p > 0.5$). With $n{=}200$ paired episodes, the minimum detectable effect is ${\sim}8$~pp ($\alpha = 0.05$, 80\% power): the key $N{=}1{\to}3$ decline is well-powered; the plateau claim ($N{=}3{\to}5$, $+1.0$~pp) is also supported, since a ${\geq}8$~pp improvement would be detected. We characterize this as suggestive evidence of a Reflexion ceiling rather than a conclusive demonstration. Retry's per-trial conditional death rate stays constant at ${\sim}48\%$; Reflexion's declines to ${\sim}31\%$ by Trial~3 (per-task breakdown in Appendix~\ref{app:dose-response}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/dose_response.pdf}
\caption{Death rate as a function of trial count ($N$) for Retry and Reflexion on 10 death-prone tasks ($n{=}200$). Retry (blue) is nearly flat; Reflexion (red) drops steeply through $N{=}3$ then plateaus. Horizontal lines: 72B scaling baseline ($30.6\%$, all 11 tasks) and deterministic baseline ($16.3\%$). Error bands: Wilson 95\% CIs.}
\label{fig:dose-response}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Environment Boundary Probes}
\label{sec:math-contrast}
\label{sec:cross-env}

We examine MATH and ALFWorld as boundary probes---not cross-domain validation---testing framework applicability across environments that instantiate ``deterministic computation'' differently (oracle simulator, code interpreter, admissible-action enumeration).

\paragraph{MATH.} On MATH Level~4--5~\citep{math-2021} ($N{=}795$, code interpreter), internal reasoning and code interpretation achieve near-identical accuracy ($73.5\%$ vs.\ $73.0\%$, McNemar $p = 0.81$), with 74.6\% no-preference. Without catastrophic failure, no routing signal emerges.

\paragraph{ALFWorld.} ALFWorld~\citep{alfworld-2021} (6 task types, 135 games) provides the deterministic strategy with admissible actions---an information asymmetry rather than a computation mode switch, with no death mechanism ($\delta = 0$, DAF $= 0\%$). With Qwen2.5-7B, 35 of 37 non-tie labels (94.6\%) favor the information-advantaged strategy (Appendix~\ref{app:cross-env-details}); Llama-3.1-8B (135 games) yields 16 of 16 non-tie labels in the same direction (100\%). Llama shows fewer non-tie episodes (11.9\% vs.\ 25.9\%) but perfect directional consistency, confirming that information asymmetry---not death avoidance---drives routing signal in ALFWorld regardless of model family. The framework diagnoses this qualitatively different mechanism (DAF $= 0\%$ vs.\ $90.4\%$) without modification.

\subsection{Baselines}
\label{sec:baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lccc@{}}
\toprule
Strategy & Score & 95\% CI & Death \% \\
\midrule
Always-Internal & $-39.07$ & [$-46.40$, $-32.27$] & 42.9 \\
Always-Deterministic & $-9.47$ & [$-14.71$, $-4.29$] & 16.3 \\
Random & $-22.38$ & [$-28.67$, $-15.75$] & 27.8 \\
Oracle & $-6.58$ & [$-11.54$, $-1.70$] & 14.3 \\
\midrule
Trained Router$^\dagger$ & $-10.70$ & [$-16.33$, $-5.27$] & 17.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Episode-weighted mean scores on ScienceWorld (11 task types, 20--40 episodes per type, 245 total). $\dagger$\,Single random seed (42) per episode pair; see Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness} for 8-seed robustness analysis.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\end{table}

Always-deterministic ($-9.47$) outperforms always-internal ($-39.07$) by 29.60 points. The oracle improves only to $-6.58$, with 96.3\% of decisions matching always-deterministic---an \emph{oracle degeneracy} where an always-deterministic lookup achieves 90.5\% of non-tie oracle decisions (balanced accuracy 50.0\%: perfect deterministic recall, zero internal recall).

\subsection{Router Training: A Negative Result}
\label{sec:router-diagnostic}

We probe whether counterfactual labels can train a step-level router, as a diagnostic of label informativeness. The oracle margin (2.89 points) caps routing upside. The near-chance step-level accuracy admits two interpretations: (A)~episode-level labels projected onto steps create ${\sim}$88\% uninformative signal (60.3\% pre-event neutral, 27.4\% post-event), drowning learnable patterns in noise; (B)~routing signal is genuinely task-type-level---there is no step-level pattern to learn. Directional conflict is only 0.3\% of 2{,}040 training steps, but the granularity mismatch leaves minimal signal. The data scaling ablation (25\%--800\%, no improvement) and extreme class imbalance (86/95 $= 90.5\%$ deterministic-preferred) together favor Interpretation~B (non-learnability): routing signal is genuinely task-type-level, not step-level learnable---though we cannot definitively rule out Interpretation~A.

An implicit router (Qwen3, LoRA; Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}) trained at three scales (0.6B, 1.7B, 4B) shows flat step-level balanced accuracy ($56$--$59\%$). At episode level, only 4B exceeds always-deterministic ($59.0 \pm 1.9\%$, $p < 0.005$); 0.6B and 1.7B remain at chance. Episode-level independent evaluation yields $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below the majority-vote baseline of 50.0\%; this independent set includes held-out task types, so the below-chance result reflects cross-task transfer failure rather than overall router incompetence. Training uses episode-level splits (Appendix~\ref{app:router-details}), ensuring no within-episode leakage. The high uninformative rate connects to noisy-label learning~\citep{natarajan2013learning}: standard ERM on labels with ${\sim}$88\% noise yields near-chance classifiers, consistent with our results.

\paragraph{End-to-end deployment.} The 4B router deployed end-to-end ($N{=}245$) scores $-12.03$ (95\% CI: $[-17.82, -6.45]$), not exceeding always-deterministic ($-9.47$). Paraphrase-based augmentation ($25\%$--$800\%$) provides no improvement; the $3.7\%$ internal-preferred base rate makes real-data collection expensive (Appendices~\ref{app:seed-robustness}--\ref{app:ablations}).

\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Why the router fails: task-type-level signal.}
The router diagnostic (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) reveals a structural mismatch: death avoidance is a task-type-level binary property (10/11 types carry death risk), but step-level features correlate weakly because death risk depends on multi-step trajectories. The 4B router improves at episode level ($59.0\%$) without step-level gains ($56$--$59\%$), confirming task-type classification; cross-task-type transfer yields chance accuracy (49.2\%; Appendix~\ref{app:ablations}).

\paragraph{Non-triviality of death dominance.}
While death dominance follows from the penalty structure (\S\ref{sec:daf}), three specific consequences were not predictable a priori: (i)~$10{\times}$ scaling to 72B fails to eliminate it (DAF${}= 92.4\%$); (ii)~Reflexion self-correction yields DAF${}>100\%$, meaning death avoidance \emph{more} than accounts for the full gap; (iii)~a 0.5B model shows \emph{reduced} death rate ($13.3\%$; $n{=}15$) because it is too weak to reach dangerous states, revealing an inverted-U capability--vulnerability relationship. All four intervention axes leave ${\geq}12$~pp residual gaps to deterministic safety (Appendix~\ref{sec:threshold-routing}); cross-model stability ($36$--$43\%$ internal DR across three families) confirms death vulnerability is a task-environment property. Our primary models differ in pretraining data, tokenizer, alignment, and attention mechanism (Gemma-2: sliding window vs.\ full attention in Qwen/Llama), providing diversity beyond parameter count; 72B scaling confirms persistence beyond this range. More aggressive interventions remain untested, so we characterize a ceiling, not a universal bound.

\paragraph{Anatomy of catastrophic failure.}
All 105 internal-strategy deaths trace to misuse of \texttt{focus on [object]}, the answer-submission action, as a general interaction command (Appendix~\ref{app:failure-modes}). The root cause is grounding failure: agents parse task descriptions correctly but cannot map understanding to valid primitives. The internal strategy's higher death count (105 vs.\ 40) reflects more frequent entry into catastrophic states, not different within-episode behavior (${\sim}5\%$ \texttt{focus on} rate in both). Direct grounding interventions reduce but do not eliminate vulnerability ($48.0\% \to 29.0\%$; Appendix~\ref{app:grounding-ablation}).

\paragraph{Practical value as meta-diagnostic.}
The framework's primary value is meta-diagnostic: a ${\sim}$50-episode pilot (Appendix~\ref{app:decision-framework}) answers three questions for any dual-strategy environment---does routing signal exist, what mechanism drives it, and is learned-router development warranted? Across three environments, it yields qualitatively distinct diagnoses: ScienceWorld (DAF${=}90.4\%$) identifies death dominance, prescribing always-deterministic; ALFWorld (DAF${=}0\%$) identifies information asymmetry from admissible-action access; MATH ($p{=}0.81$) finds no routing signal. The pilot correctly predicted all four tested conditions, reframing our 200 GPU-hour router training as a validated negative result---transforming the open question ``should we build a router?'' into a quantitative decision.

\paragraph{Framework applicability.}
The penalty-independent label fraction (78.9\%) is the primary diagnostic; DAF increases monotonically with $P$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). Boundary conditions confirm mechanism absence or universality (0\% and 100\% death asymmetry respectively). NetHack~\citep{nethack-2020} combat tasks remain the most direct replication target~\citep{balrog-2025,netplay-2024}.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

Our diagnostic framework quantifies what drives routing signal in interactive environments through three components: (i)~episode-level counterfactual labeling that generates routing preferences from paired pure-strategy outcomes (\S\ref{sec:collection}--\ref{sec:labeling}); (ii)~the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF), which decomposes the routing score gap into death-avoidance and non-death components (\S\ref{sec:daf}); and (iii)~the Signal Concentration Index (SCI), which measures whether routing signal is concentrated in a few task types or distributed systematically (Appendix~\ref{app:sci}). Episode-level bootstrap resampling provides 95\% confidence intervals for all metrics.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Consider an interactive environment with observation space $\mathcal{O}$ and action space $\mathcal{A}$. An agent policy $\pi: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{A}$ maps observation histories to actions. We define two computation modes $\mathcal{M} = \{{\tt int}, {\tt det}\}$: \textbf{internal} (${\tt int}$), where the agent predicts intermediate observations via chain-of-thought reasoning (ReAct~\cite{react-2023}), with prediction errors compounding across steps; and \textbf{deterministic}\footnote{The term ``deterministic'' denotes different information advantages across environments: in ScienceWorld, an oracle simulator providing ground-truth action outcomes; in MATH, a code interpreter verifying computations; in ALFWorld, admissible-action enumeration eliminating hallucination risk. These qualitative differences affect what routing signals the framework can detect.} (${\tt det}$), where the agent receives ground-truth observations from the environment simulator at each step. A \emph{routing policy} $\rho: \mathcal{O}^* \rightarrow \mathcal{M}$ selects which computation mode to use at each step; our analysis investigates what signal is available to learn $\rho$.

\subsection{Counterfactual Episode Collection}
\label{sec:collection}

Per-step oracle labeling assigns routing labels by comparing single-step outcomes under each computation mode. Under sparse terminal rewards, this approach degenerates: on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, 97.4\% of steps produce identical intermediate scores regardless of computation mode (5{,}726 of 5{,}878 steps across 11 prescreened task types), yielding no per-step routing signal. The 97.4\% tie rate arises because ScienceWorld assigns rewards only at episode termination; intermediate steps update environment state but do not change the score, so a per-step oracle can only distinguish strategies through downstream consequences visible at episode end. Episode-level comparison is therefore the principled granularity for sparse-reward environments, not a fallback from per-step analysis: it directly measures whether one strategy produces better terminal outcomes than the other, bypassing the uninformative intermediate steps entirely.

\paragraph{Pure-strategy trajectories.}
For a task instance $t$ and computation mode $m \in \mathcal{M}$, a \emph{pure-strategy trajectory} is the complete episode obtained by applying mode $m$ at every step:
\begin{equation}
    \tau_m^{(t)} = (o_1, a_1, o_2, a_2, \ldots, o_T, a_T)
\end{equation}
where $T \leq T_{\max}$ is the episode length (capped at $T_{\max} = 30$ steps). Both trajectories $\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}$ and $\tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}$ are executed under identical initial conditions (same random seed, same task variation), so performance differences arise solely from the computation mode choice.

Each trajectory $\tau$ yields a terminal score $s(\tau) \in \mathbb{Z}$ and a death indicator $d(\tau) \in \{0, 1\}$. When the agent dies ($d = 1$), the environment imposes a penalty: $s(\tau) = -P$ where $P > 0$ ($P = 100$ in ScienceWorld). The episode pair produces a tuple:
\begin{multline}
    \bigl(s^{\tt int}, s^{\tt det}, d^{\tt int}, d^{\tt det}\bigr) \triangleq \\
    \bigl(s(\tau_{\tt int}),\, s(\tau_{\tt det}),\, d(\tau_{\tt int}),\, d(\tau_{\tt det})\bigr)
\end{multline}

We use Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct~\citep{qwen25-2024} (vLLM) on ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a text-based environment spanning 30 task types with a $-100$ death penalty. After prescreening, we collect 245 episode pairs across 11 task types (20--40 per type). The framework is applicable to any environment where paired episodes under two computation modes can be collected with binary success/failure indicators. We apply it unchanged to ALFWorld and MATH in \S\ref{sec:math-contrast}--\ref{sec:cross-env}.

\subsection{Episode-Level Labeling}
\label{sec:labeling}

We define a labeling function $L$ that maps a counterfactual pair to a routing preference:
\begin{multline}
    L\!\bigl(\tau_{\tt int}^{(t)}, \tau_{\tt det}^{(t)}\bigr) \;\rightarrow \\
    \ell \in \{{\tt det\text{-}pref},\; {\tt int\text{-}pref},\; {\tt no\text{-}pref}\}
\end{multline}

The labeling function is \emph{death-aware}: it prioritizes survival over score, then compares scores among same-status episodes (Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}; Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}). ScienceWorld scores are integer-valued, so we use exact (in)equality with no threshold. Figure~\ref{fig:pipeline} illustrates the overall pipeline; label distributions are reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/pipeline_overview.pdf}
\caption{Counterfactual episode collection and analysis pipeline. Each task instance is run under both pure strategies independently, producing episode-level labels via Algorithm~\ref{alg:labeling}.}
\label{fig:pipeline}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Death Avoidance Factor}
\label{sec:daf}

To quantify the contribution of death avoidance to routing signal, we decompose the mean score gap between strategies. Let $\delta = \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt int}] - \mathbb{E}[d^{\tt det}]$ denote the death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ the mean score gap restricted to episodes where neither strategy dies. Partitioning episodes by death status, death-asymmetric episodes (where only one strategy dies) contribute a per-episode gap of $P + \bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$, where $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}}$ is the surviving strategy's mean score; when $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} \ll P$ (in our data, $\bar{s}_{\text{surv}} = 0.87$, or $0.9\%$ of $P$), these episodes contribute ${\approx}\,\delta P$ in aggregate. The full gap thus decomposes as:
\begin{equation}
    \Delta_{\text{full}} \approx \underbrace{\delta \cdot P}_{\text{death contribution}} + \Delta_{\text{nd}}
\end{equation}
The approximation residual is $29.60 - 29.37 = 0.23$ ($0.8\%$ of $\Delta_{\text{full}}$).

We define the Death Avoidance Factor (DAF) as the fraction of the full score gap attributable to death avoidance:
\begin{equation}
    \text{DAF} = 1 - \frac{\Delta_{\text{nd}}}{\Delta_{\text{full}}} \approx \frac{\delta P}{\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}}
\end{equation}%
\footnote{When $\Delta_{\text{nd}} < 0$ (deterministic strategy scores lower than internal on non-death episodes), DAF exceeds 1; death avoidance then overcompensates for a non-death disadvantage.}

Since DAF $= \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}})$, it increases monotonically with $P$, a mathematical consequence rather than an empirical finding. We therefore report both DAF at the environment default ($P{=}100$) and the penalty-independent death-asymmetry label fraction as complementary metrics. In ScienceWorld, $P$ affects only post-hoc score calculation; the agent's policy (ReAct prompting) does not include $P$ in its observation, so death rates and $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ are $P$-invariant by design (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}).

\paragraph{Relationship between DAF and label fraction.}
DAF (90.4\%) exceeds the label fraction (78.9\%) because death episodes carry disproportionately large score gaps ($P{=}100 \gg$ typical non-death $\Delta{\approx}29$), amplifying their weight in score-weighted aggregation. The label fraction---invariant to penalty magnitude---serves as the primary finding; DAF reflects the score-space reality under ScienceWorld's reward structure.

\paragraph{Statistical inference.}
We quantify uncertainty via episode-level bootstrap resampling ($B = 10{,}000$). Each resample draws $N = 245$ episodes with replacement, recomputes all metrics, and derives percentile-based 95\% CIs. We resample at the episode level (not the step level) because the episode is the unit of counterfactual comparison. Penalty sensitivity analysis across hypothetical $P$ values is in Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}.

We also measure whether death-avoidance signal is concentrated in a few task types via a Signal Concentration Index (SCI $\in [0, 1]$; Appendix~\ref{app:sci}), reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp}.

\paragraph{Scope of episode-level analysis.}
At step level, 97.4\% of oracle comparisons yield ties (\S\ref{sec:collection}); of 2{,}040 training steps, only 0.3\% exhibit strict directional conflict with episode labels, with 60.3\% pre-event neutral (neither strategy advantaged). Episode labels thus apply correct direction to uninformative steps---a granularity mismatch, not label error---explaining why step-level router training approaches chance performance (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}).

